<section xml:id="sec-mod5-worked-examples">
  <title>Worked Examples</title>
  <p>Estimated time: 40 minutes.</p>

    <p>
      Read each example slowly. The goal is not just to see what the
      answer is, but to internalize the proof <em>template</em>.
      <xref ref="ex-mod5-we-1"/> is the archetype of a direct partial-sums argument
      (telescoping); <xref ref="ex-mod5-we-2"/> shows the Limit Comparison Test in
      action; <xref ref="ex-mod5-we-3"/> demonstrates the Ratio Test on a factorial
      series; <xref ref="ex-mod5-we-4"/> treats conditional convergence via the
      Alternating Series Test.
    </p>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 1: TELESCOPING FROM PARTIAL SUMS
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod5-we-1">
    <title>A Telescoping Series from Partial Sums</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove directly from the definition of series convergence that
        <md>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{k(k+1)} = 1.</md>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
        To prove a series equals a specific number, we must show its
        partial-sum sequence <m>(S_n)</m> converges to that number.
        Here we will find an explicit closed form for <m>S_n</m> and
        then take a limit using <xref ref="ch-mod4"/> tools.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Scratch work.</alert>
        The summand admits the partial-fraction decomposition
        <md>\frac{1}{k(k+1)} = \frac{1}{k} - \frac{1}{k+1},</md>
        which one checks by combining the right-hand side over a
        common denominator. The intermediate terms in the partial
        sum will cancel in pairs.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
        Using the partial-fraction identity, the <m>n</m>th partial sum is
        <md>
          <mrow>S_n = \sum_{k=1}^{n} \frac{1}{k(k+1)} \amp= \sum_{k=1}^{n} \left(\frac{1}{k} - \frac{1}{k+1}\right)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \left(1 - \frac{1}{2}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{2} - \frac{1}{3}\right) + \cdots + \left(\frac{1}{n} - \frac{1}{n+1}\right)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= 1 - \frac{1}{n + 1},</mrow>
        </md>
        since all intermediate terms cancel. By the Algebra of Limits
        (<xref ref="ch-mod4"/>) together with the standard limit <m>1/(n+1) \to 0</m>,
        <md>\lim_{n \to \infty} S_n
        = \lim_{n \to \infty} \left(1 - \frac{1}{n + 1}\right)
        = 1 - 0 = 1.</md>
        By the definition of series convergence,
        <m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{k(k+1)} = 1</m>.
        <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        This is the <em>only</em> worked example in which the
        sum is computed <em>exactly</em> from the definition. Two
        features were critical: (1) a closed form for the summand
        (partial fractions) that made the cancellation visible, and
        (2) a closed form for <m>S_n</m> that reduced the problem to
        a <xref ref="ch-mod4"/> limit. When neither is available, which is
        nearly always the case, you must use convergence
        <em>tests</em> to determine whether a limit exists, without
        being able to identify its value.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 2: LIMIT COMPARISON TEST
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod5-we-2">
    <title>The Limit Comparison Test</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether
        <md>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{k^2 + k}</md>
        converges. Use the Limit Comparison Test. You may take as
        known that <m>\sum 1/k^2</m> converges (this is Zorn's
        <m>p</m>-series theorem with <m>p = 2</m>).
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
        For large <m>k</m>, the summand <m>1/(k^2 + k)</m> behaves
        like <m>1/k^2</m>, so we compare to <m>\sum 1/k^2</m>. Here
        direct comparison would also work, since
        <m>1/(k^2 + k) \leq 1/k^2</m> for every <m>k</m>; we use the
        Limit Comparison Test to show the method. Its advantage is that
        it needs no explicit inequality <m>a_k \leq b_k</m>, which
        matters when the natural inequality points the wrong way: for
        <m>\sum 1/(k^2 - k + 1)</m>, every term is at least
        <m>1/k^2</m>, so direct comparison with <m>\sum 1/k^2</m> needs
        a new bound, while the ratio of terms still tends to 1.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
        Set <m>a_k = \dfrac{1}{k^2 + k}</m> and <m>b_k = \dfrac{1}{k^2}</m>.
        Both are positive for <m>k \geq 1</m>. We compute
        <md>\frac{a_k}{b_k} = \frac{1/(k^2 + k)}{1/k^2}
        = \frac{k^2}{k^2 + k} = \frac{1}{1 + 1/k}.</md>
        By the quotient rule from the Algebra of Limits and the
        standard limit <m>1/k \to 0</m>,
        <md>\lim_{k \to \infty} \frac{a_k}{b_k}
        = \frac{1}{1 + 0} = 1.</md>
        Since the limit is finite and nonzero, the Limit Comparison
        Test applies: <m>\sum a_k</m> and <m>\sum b_k</m> either both
        converge or both diverge. Because <m>\sum 1/k^2</m> converges,
        so does
        <m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{k^2 + k}</m>.
        <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        The rhythm of the proof is standard: (1) identify a
        <q>known</q> series with similar-looking large-<m>k</m> behavior;
        (2) compute the ratio of summands and take its limit; (3)
        verify the limit lies in <m>(0, \infty)</m>; (4) invoke the
        test by name. If the ratio limit had been <m>0</m> or <m>\infty</m>,
        a weaker conclusion would follow (see Zorn's exact statement
        of Theorem 2.29), but the case of a finite nonzero limit is
        the most commonly useful. The hypothesis of <em>positive
        terms</em> must be checked: Limit Comparison is a test for
        positive series, so the proof notes explicitly that <m>a_k, b_k \gt 0</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 3: RATIO TEST
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod5-we-3">
    <title>The Ratio Test on a Factorial Series</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether
        <md>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{k!}{k^k}</md>
        converges. Use the Ratio Test.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
        Series involving factorials are almost always attacked with
        the Ratio Test, because the factorial and exponential ratios
        simplify cleanly. We compute <m>\lim_{k \to \infty} a_{k+1}/a_k</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
        Let <m>a_k = k!/k^k \gt 0</m>. Then
        <md>
          <mrow>\frac{a_{k+1}}{a_k} \amp= \frac{(k+1)!}{(k+1)^{k+1}} \cdot \frac{k^k}{k!}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{(k+1) \cdot k!}{(k+1) \cdot (k+1)^k} \cdot \frac{k^k}{k!}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{k^k}{(k+1)^k} = \left(\frac{k}{k+1}\right)^k = \frac{1}{\left(1 + \frac{1}{k}\right)^k}.</mrow>
        </md>
        It is a standard limit (proved in Zorn &#xa7;2.3 or its
        problem set; also a limit you know from calculus) that
        <m>\left(1 + \frac{1}{k}\right)^k \to e</m> as <m>k \to \infty</m>.
        By the quotient rule,
        <md>\lim_{k \to \infty} \frac{a_{k+1}}{a_k}
        = \frac{1}{e} \approx 0.368.</md>
        Since <m>1/e \lt 1</m>, the Ratio Test implies
        <m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{k!}{k^k}</m>
        converges. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        The algebraic simplification in the second step is the whole
        game: <m>(k+1)!/k! = k+1</m>, and <m>(k+1)^{k+1}/(k+1)^k = k+1</m>,
        so a single factor of <m>(k+1)</m> in numerator and denominator
        cancels and leaves a clean ratio. Recognizing the
        <m>(1 + 1/k)^k</m> pattern, rather than fumbling with
        binomial expansions, is what makes the test feel
        routine. The Ratio Test delivers a yes/no verdict only; it
        does not tell you what the sum is, and nothing in this module
        gives the value of <m>\sum k!/k^k</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 4: ALTERNATING SERIES & CONDITIONAL CONVERGENCE
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod5-we-4">
    <title>Conditional Convergence via the Alternating Series Test</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that
        <md>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^{k+1}}{\sqrt{k}}</md>
        converges, and then show that it does <em>not</em> converge
        absolutely. Conclude that the series converges conditionally.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
        Absolute convergence is the stronger condition, so we handle
        it in stages. First, use the Alternating Series Test to get
        convergence. Then, look at <m>\sum |a_k| = \sum 1/\sqrt{k}</m>
        and show it diverges via comparison with the harmonic series
        (or directly as a <m>p</m>-series with <m>p = 1/2 \lt 1</m>).
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Step 1: Convergence via the Alternating Series Test (AST).</alert>
        Write the series as <m>\sum (-1)^{k+1} b_k</m> with
        <m>b_k = 1/\sqrt{k} \gt 0</m>. To apply the Alternating Series
        Test we verify two conditions:
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>(i) <m>(b_k)</m> is decreasing.</em> For <m>k \in \N</m>,
        <m>\sqrt{k} \lt \sqrt{k+1}</m>, so <m>1/\sqrt{k} \gt 1/\sqrt{k+1}</m>,
        i.e., <m>b_k \gt b_{k+1}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>(ii) <m>b_k \to 0</m>.</em> For any <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>,
        by the Archimedean Property pick <m>N \gt 1/\varepsilon^2</m>.
        Then for <m>k \geq N</m>, <m>\sqrt{k} \geq \sqrt{N} \gt 1/\varepsilon</m>,
        so <m>b_k = 1/\sqrt{k} \lt \varepsilon</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both hypotheses of the Alternating Series Test are satisfied,
        so <m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^{k+1}}{\sqrt{k}}</m>
        converges.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Step 2: Absolute divergence.</alert>
        Consider the series of absolute values,
        <m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{\sqrt{k}}</m>.
        We show this diverges by direct comparison with the harmonic
        series. Since <m>\sqrt{k} \leq k</m> for all <m>k \in \N</m>,
        <md>\frac{1}{k} \leq \frac{1}{\sqrt{k}}.</md>
        The harmonic series <m>\sum 1/k</m> diverges
        (<xref ref="ex-mod5-sg-25-4"/>). By the contrapositive
        form of the Comparison Test,
        <m>\sum 1/\sqrt{k}</m> also diverges.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Conclusion.</alert>
        The series <m>\sum (-1)^{k+1}/\sqrt{k}</m> converges (Step 1)
        but not absolutely (Step 2). By definition, it converges
        <em>conditionally</em>. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        The proof template for classifying an alternating series has
        three parts: (1) apply AST to establish convergence, carefully
        verifying both hypotheses on <m>b_k</m>; (2) check absolute
        convergence separately, usually via comparison with a known
        series; (3) declare whether the convergence is absolute or
        conditional. The order matters: <em>you cannot conclude
        conditional convergence from AST alone</em>; you must
        also rule out absolute convergence. Conditional convergence
        is a delicate property: rearranging the terms of a
        conditionally convergent series can change its sum (Riemann's
        Rearrangement Theorem, a topic for MAT 5610; see, for example,
        Rudin, <pubtitle>Principles of Mathematical Analysis</pubtitle>, 3rd ed.,
        Theorem 3.54).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
